WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:00.950
MAX SALTONSTALL:
This week, we're

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:03.242
talking about the essentials
of reliability engineering

00:00:03.242 --> 00:00:05.520
and how to improve identity
and access management.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.420
I'm Max Saltonstall.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.378
Welcome to "This Week in
Cloud," where we serve

00:00:08.378 --> 00:00:10.480
you the lowest latency news.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.090
So your app is
scaling up swiftly

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.410
to meet audience adoption.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.370
Great.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.770
But continuing to add
engineers to your ops team--

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.407
not so great.

00:00:18.407 --> 00:00:20.490
This week, hear the story
of how Google originally

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.110
solved this problem
as our own network

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.090
grew by developing
an intuitive set

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.413
of site-reliability
engineering principles.

00:00:26.413 --> 00:00:28.080
Then spare yourself
the trial and error,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.210
and learn from our mistakes.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.043
When it comes to
application security,

00:00:32.043 --> 00:00:33.960
your safety relies on
managing who in your org

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.390
gets access to which resources.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.030
But the right answers
aren't always obvious.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.370
Read about applying the
principles of least privilege

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.350
to improve your
security posture.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.300
Learn our top tips for
minimizing the surface

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.310
area of exposed
applications, and see

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.980
which can make the biggest
difference for you.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.620
Speaking of access, the new
Identity and Access Management

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.780
recommender for GCP
can automatically

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.930
detect overly permissive
access policies.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.300
Then it generates
suggested adjustments

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.340
to your permissions
based on the access

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.722
patterns of similar people
in your organization.

00:01:04.722 --> 00:01:06.930
Read the full article to
learn how recommenders work,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.880
then try them out yourself.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.490
The discovery and
assessment phase

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.230
of cloud migration
projects tends

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.527
to predict whether the
migration will succeed or fail.

00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:18.360
Read this post to learn
about two tools that

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.650
can make a big difference--

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.073
StratoZone and RISC Networks.

00:01:22.073 --> 00:01:23.490
Then head over to
the walkthroughs

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.817
to see how each one
works in practice.

00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:27.900
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.190
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.270
Have any questions?

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:32.830
Leave a comment or tweet us.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.600
We'll see you in the cloud.

